2026-09-16T10:06:41.2172507Z       "body": "`tests/test_dependency_contextmanager.py` keeps a module-level `state` dict (and an `errors` list) that the app's yield dependencies mutate in place. Four tests open by asserting the pristine value, so they only pass if nothing else in the file ran first. The file passes in definition order but any other order breaks it:\n\n```console\n$ pytest tests/test_dependency_contextmanager.py::test_sync_async_state tests/test_dependency_contextmanager.py::test_async_state\n```\n\n```\ndef test_async_state():\n>       assert state[\"/async\"] == \"asyncgen not started\"\nE       AssertionError: assert 'asyncgen completed' == 'asyncgen not started'\n\ntests/test_dependency_contextmanager.py:217: AssertionError\n```\n\npytest honors CLI order, so naming the two tests in reversed order is enough to trigger it. Running the whole file in reverse definition order fails 4 of 22 tests (`test_async_state`, `test_sync_state`, `test_async_raise_other`, `test_sync_raise_other`), and random shuffles (for example under pytest-randomly) fail 2 to 4 tests depending on the seed.\n\nThis PR resets the shared state between tests with an autouse fixture, the same pattern `tests/test_dependency_yield_except_httpexception.py` already uses for the same reason. The file now passes in definition order, reversed, and shuffled. The reset cannot change what the existing tests check: every `errors` assertion is a membership check after the test's own request, and every `state` assertion reads keys the test itself transitions.\n\nTests only, no library changes.\n",
